NAME
       frequency - frequency envelopes, resonance spectra, and frequency-domain
                   analysis for triadic design in RTT

SYNOPSIS
       Frequency-domain analysis provides:
           • resonance spectra
           • frequency envelopes
           • spectral signatures
           • harmonic distribution
           • triadic design guidance

DESCRIPTION
       Frequency describes how resonance-time oscillates across cycles. While
       gradients measure change and harmonics measure oscillatory modes,
       frequency measures the *distribution* of resonance energy across the
       spectral domain.

       Frequency-domain analysis reveals:
           • which patterns are stable
           • which overlays are appropriate
           • how resonance propagates
           • when triads should morph
           • how multi-agent systems synchronize

       Frequency is evaluated by WRSADC and recorded in lineage.

FREQUENCY ENVELOPES
       A frequency envelope describes the amplitude of resonance across a
       range of frequencies. It is the “shape” of resonance in the spectral
       domain.

       Envelope types:

           narrowband
                  Most energy concentrated around a single frequency.
                  → stable, predictable triads
                  → sequential, core, or weave patterns

           broadband
                  Energy spread across many frequencies.
                  → adaptive, dynamic systems
                  → spiral, ladder, or expansion patterns

           multimodal
                  Multiple peaks at distinct frequencies.
                  → hybrid or fused patterns
                  → constellation behavior

           harmonic-clustered
                  Peaks at integer multiples.
                  → harmonic-sync networks
                  → multi-agent resonance coupling

       Envelopes guide pattern selection and overlay application.

RESONANCE SPECTRA
       A resonance spectrum is the full frequency-domain representation of
       resonance-time. It shows:

           • fundamental frequency
           • harmonic distribution
           • overtone structure
           • spectral density
           • noise components

       Spectra determine:
           • pattern viability
           • harmonic compatibility
           • multi-agent sync potential
           • resonance stability

       Spectra are derived from lineage snapshots and triad sampling.

SPECTRAL SIGNATURES
       Each triad, operator, and pattern has a characteristic spectral
       signature.

       Examples:

           sequential
                  Strong fundamental, minimal harmonics.

           spiral
                  Rising harmonic content; broadband envelope.

           ladder
                  Stepwise harmonic transitions; clustered peaks.

           expansion
                  High-frequency spread; multimodal structure.

           mirror
                  Spectral inversion; polarity flip.

           weave
                  Interleaved frequency bands.

           constellation
                  Multi-agent harmonic coupling; spectral braiding.

       Spectral signatures help WRSADC choose safe transitions.

FREQUENCY-DOMAIN TRIAD ANALYSIS
       Triads act as discrete sampling points for frequency analysis.

           P1 (begin)
               → capture baseline frequency state

           P2 (transform)
               → apply operator resonance signatures
               → update spectral distribution

           P3 (close)
               → integrate spectral changes into lineage

       Frequency analysis determines whether triads remain coherent.

FREQUENCY AND PATTERN DESIGN
       Frequency-domain analysis guides triadic design:

           narrowband → sequential, core
           broadband → spiral, ladder
           multimodal → hybrid or fused patterns
           harmonic-clustered → harmonic-sync or constellation
           high-frequency noise → anchor or mirror overlays

       Patterns must match spectral conditions to remain stable.

FREQUENCY AND OVERLAYS
       Overlays modify frequency behavior:

           telescope
                  Expands or contracts spectral range.

           mirror
                  Inverts frequency distribution.

           anchor
                  Dampens high-frequency noise.

           lens
                  Reframes spectral interpretation.

       Overlay stacks produce composite spectral effects.

FREQUENCY IN MULTI-AGENT CONSTELLATIONS
       Constellations require frequency compatibility:

           • shared fundamental frequency
           • harmonic alignment
           • stable spectral envelopes
           • compatible resonance signatures

       Frequency coupling determines whether agents can synchronize.

FREQUENCY FAILURE MODES
       spectral drift
              Frequency peaks shift unpredictably.

       overtone overload
              Harmonics exceed safe thresholds.

       spectral fragmentation
              Envelope splits into incoherent bands.

       desync
              Agents lose frequency alignment.

       WRSADC detects and prevents all failure modes.

INTERACTION WITH SCHEMAS
       Schemas define:
           • allowed spectral ranges
           • harmonic constraints
           • envelope limits
           • coupling rules

       Frequency behavior must satisfy:
           • resonance-schema
           • structural-schema
           • constellation-schema

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:
           • spectral snapshots
           • envelope evolution
           • harmonic transitions
           • frequency coupling events

       Frequency analysis depends on lineage continuity.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • computes spectral signatures
           • validates frequency envelopes
           • selects patterns based on spectra
           • applies overlays to stabilize frequency
           • blocks unsafe spectral states

       WRSADC is the spectral analyst.

INTERACTION WITH THE ENGINE
       The Engine:
           • executes frequency-sensitive operators
           • updates internal RT-frequency state
           • integrates spectral effects

       The Engine assumes spectral safety; WRSADC guarantees it.

NOTES
       Frequency is the spectral dimension of resonance-time. It reveals the
       hidden structure of triads, patterns, and constellations, guiding safe
       and coherent triadic design across the entire RTT system.

